WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:06.039
- [Kirby] When that water comes
in, it changes everything.

00:00:06.039 --> 00:00:10.800
- [Krisha] It just kept dumping
rain for hours and days.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.029
It was heart-wrenching.

00:00:13.029 --> 00:00:16.480
- [Priscilla] It rose
overnight very quickly.

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:17.800
- [Nathan] It's pretty mind-boggling,

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:19.469
in more ways than one.

00:00:19.469 --> 00:00:22.251
It was a pretty emotional
moment, to say the least.

00:00:22.251 --> 00:00:24.039
- [Greg] People are worried
about their families

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:26.038
and their loved ones so I
started thinking about,

00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:27.981
how can I help with this?

00:00:27.981 --> 00:00:29.280
- [Woman On Radio] The office
of Emergency Management

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.480
is calling on owners of
any powered water craft

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.421
in Greater Houston to assist in the rescue

00:00:33.421 --> 00:00:35.950
of those trapped in flooded areas.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:37.510
- When we woke up that morning,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.746
we discovered that we are an island.

00:00:39.746 --> 00:00:40.621
All the major highways,

00:00:40.621 --> 00:00:42.401
all the major roadways were underwater.

00:00:42.401 --> 00:00:43.647
The only way we could talk to each other

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:45.478
was mainly through social media,

00:00:45.478 --> 00:00:47.217
and so we created a Facebook group

00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:49.449
called the Fairfield Relief Effort.

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.878
- My brother actually
saw something on Facebook

00:00:51.878 --> 00:00:53.929
saying that 'we need all
the help we can get.'

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:57.248
Any flat bottom boat or shallow
water boat that can come assist.

00:00:57.248 --> 00:00:59.766
We just kinda looked at each
other and the rest is history.

00:00:59.766 --> 00:01:03.198
We got home, loaded up the
boat, and headed up that way.

00:01:03.198 --> 00:01:05.776
- There was a line of
people just wanting to give --

00:01:05.776 --> 00:01:07.800
to either go do rescues or donate.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.430
Anything to help the others.

00:01:09.430 --> 00:01:10.710
It was so overwhelming,

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.648
the amount of people
wanting to support us.

00:01:12.648 --> 00:01:15.128
We had well over 200+ volunteers,

00:01:15.128 --> 00:01:18.367
and we've rescued over 125 people.

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:20.008
- [Nathan] We got in touch with Delmar,

00:01:20.008 --> 00:01:21.358
and, you know, we asked, "Is there
anything we can do?

00:01:21.358 --> 00:01:23.368
"Do you need help or anything like that?"

00:01:23.368 --> 00:01:24.350
We took him back to his house

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.629
and he was able to save a few things.

00:01:26.629 --> 00:01:29.657
- Austin came up here and
helped me pick everything up.

00:01:29.657 --> 00:01:33.059
They are examples to
everybody in this county.

00:01:33.059 --> 00:01:36.392
They take helping people to a new level.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:40.608
- Our son's kindergarten teacher, she

00:01:40.608 --> 00:01:44.400
posted online that her
in-laws needed to be rescued,

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:47.545
that their neighborhood
was flooding really fast,

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:50.679
and they needed somebody
to come down and get them.

00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:55.483
- Krisha, right away messaged me and then
they rescued my in-laws.

00:01:55.483 --> 00:01:57.182
- [Kirby] My particular
vehicle has a snorkle in it,

00:01:57.182 --> 00:02:01.411
so it's able to go into waters
a little deeper than normal.

00:02:01.411 --> 00:02:03.152
- [Krisha] There were
road closures everywhere,

00:02:03.152 --> 00:02:06.561
and their neighborhood
was completely underwater.

00:02:06.561 --> 00:02:08.472
- [Kirby] To get from Point A to Point B,

00:02:08.472 --> 00:02:12.792
we're navigating around
people in canoes and boats.

00:02:12.792 --> 00:02:14.152
- When we finally got the family

00:02:14.152 --> 00:02:17.041
back here to dry ground, if you will,

00:02:17.041 --> 00:02:20.179
you know, Mrs. Brewin, she says, "Thank you,
how can we repay you?"

00:02:20.179 --> 00:02:22.643
I was like, "Listen, you
taught my kid how to read."

00:02:22.643 --> 00:02:24.393
It's fine, forget it.

00:02:25.483 --> 00:02:27.792
- Houston is such an incredibly large city

00:02:27.792 --> 00:02:30.533
with so many people from
various backgrounds,

00:02:30.533 --> 00:02:31.824
but it didn't matter.

00:02:31.824 --> 00:02:34.024
People came together to help each other.

00:02:34.024 --> 00:02:35.973
I was in constant awe of my neighbors,

00:02:35.973 --> 00:02:37.343
of my entire community.

00:02:37.343 --> 00:02:39.282
I just got chills now.

00:02:39.282 --> 00:02:40.750
- When people are in need,

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.592
it's just an instinct that kicks in.

00:02:42.592 --> 00:02:45.602
I was just one small part of a community.

00:02:45.602 --> 00:02:46.722
- Everybody pitched in.

00:02:46.722 --> 00:02:48.032
I mean, it was a group effort.

00:02:48.032 --> 00:02:49.291
I mean, I’ve grown up in Texas;

00:02:49.291 --> 00:02:50.788
I know what people are like here,

00:02:50.788 --> 00:02:51.961
and they're pretty awesome.

00:02:51.961 --> 00:02:55.878
We're pretty good at
taking care of each other.

